winebus: a motor step nobody can feel is not worth a slot on the link.

mgvf-0010 writes the motors whenever the value a client asks for changes. On a
cable that is free. Over Bluetooth it is not: bluetoothd negotiates a 15 ms
sniff interval for a game controller, so the link carries about 65 reports a
second each way, and that ceiling does not move for any API -- synchronous or
asynchronous, one write in flight or sixteen, 12 bytes of payload or 78.

WHAT THOSE PACKETS ACTUALLY SAY. Measured over a Bluetooth session of an Unreal
title driving the pad through wine's xinput, 1951 motor packets:

    49% moved a motor by ONE step of 255
    69% by two or fewer
    76% by four or fewer

A step is 0.4% of the motor's range. Nobody's hand resolves one. Half of what
this driver spends the link on is dithering along a ramp.

XInputRumbleDeadband is the smallest change worth a packet, in steps of 255, and
it is OFF by default. At four -- 1.6% of the range -- 73% of the writes above
would not have been made.

AND IT IS NOT THE INTERVAL BY ANOTHER NAME. XInputRumbleInterval drops changes
by TIME, and time cannot tell a hammer blow from a tremble: raising it to 60 ms
made the rumble feel dead because the transitions went out with everything else.
This drops changes by SIZE. Every transition a hand can notice is sent at once;
what is held back is the part that says nothing. The interval goes back to being
what it should always have been -- a ceiling on how hard the link is worked --
rather than the control that decides how the rumble feels.

TWO RULES MAKE IT CORRECT, and both were got right on purpose.

The comparison is against the last pair the PAD WAS GIVEN, never against the
last pair a client asked for. A ramp creeping one step at a time accumulates
against what was sent, crosses the band, and goes out; measured against the last
request it would be dropped for ever and a slow build-up would never arrive.

Zero is exempt in both directions. A pad that goes on buzzing because the stop
was under the band is a defect and not a saving.

WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED. Whether four is the right number: 49%, 69% and 76% say
where the packets are, not where the threshold belongs, and only a hand on the
pad can say that. The value is per device and off by default for exactly that
reason. Nor is it established that this removes the frame cost -- what is known
is that the cost is the write (mgvf-0017 removed the write and nothing else, and
the game ran perfectly), and that fewer writes is the only lever anyone has
found. Whether 16 writes a second is under the knee where 60 was over it is the
next thing to measure, not a claim made here.

Signed-off-by: MacGameVideoFix
---
--- a/dlls/winebus.sys/main.c
+++ b/dlls/winebus.sys/main.c
@@ -136,6 +136,9 @@
     /* MGVF mgvf-0017: an INSTRUMENT, not a feature. Non-zero makes the haptics
      * thread do everything it does except reach the radio. */
     unsigned int haptics_dry_run;
+    /* MGVF mgvf-0018: how many steps of 255 a motor must move before the link
+     * is used, and the last pair actually sent. */
+    unsigned int haptics_deadband;
     /* The motor write happens on this thread and not on the caller's. `wanted`
      * is the last thing asked for, packed as rumble<<16|buzz so that it can be
      * exchanged in one instruction without a lock. */
@@ -1757,6 +1760,12 @@
     DEVICE_OBJECT *device = arg;
     struct device_extension *ext = (struct device_extension *)device->DeviceExtension;
     HANDLE waits[2] = {ext->haptics_stop, ext->haptics_wake};
+    /* MGVF mgvf-0018: the last pair the PAD was actually given, not the last one
+     * a client asked for. Comparing against what was sent is what lets a slow
+     * ramp accumulate: creeping by one step a packet, it crosses the band and
+     * goes out, so nothing is lost -- only gathered. Comparing against the last
+     * REQUEST would drop such a ramp on the floor for ever. */
+    int last_right = -1, last_left = -1;
     LONG last = -1;
 
     SetThreadDescription(GetCurrentThread(), L"wine_winebus_dualsense_haptics");
@@ -1834,6 +1843,30 @@
          * Bluetooth because the preference is applied further down, on the
          * translated packet, and the number here would otherwise read as "the
          * setting was ignored". */
+        /* MGVF mgvf-0018: is this packet worth a slot on the link?
+         *
+         * A stop is always worth one. A motor that has reached zero and is still
+         * being asked for zero would be held back by any band, and a pad that
+         * goes on buzzing after the game stopped asking is a defect, not a
+         * saving -- so zero is exempt in both directions. */
+        if (ext->haptics_deadband && last_right >= 0)
+        {
+            unsigned char r = motors[DUALSENSE_USB_COMMON + DUALSENSE_COMMON_MOTOR_RIGHT];
+            unsigned char l = motors[DUALSENSE_USB_COMMON + DUALSENSE_COMMON_MOTOR_LEFT];
+            BOOL stopping = (!r && last_right) || (!l && last_left);
+            unsigned int moved_r = r > last_right ? r - last_right : last_right - r;
+            unsigned int moved_l = l > last_left ? l - last_left : last_left - l;
+
+            if (!stopping && moved_r < ext->haptics_deadband && moved_l < ext->haptics_deadband)
+            {
+                TRACE("device %p: motors %u/%u moved %u/%u of 255, under the band of %u -- not sent\n",
+                      device, r, l, moved_r, moved_l, ext->haptics_deadband);
+                continue;
+            }
+        }
+        last_right = motors[DUALSENSE_USB_COMMON + DUALSENSE_COMMON_MOTOR_RIGHT];
+        last_left = motors[DUALSENSE_USB_COMMON + DUALSENSE_COMMON_MOTOR_LEFT];
+
         TRACE("device %p: the packet carries motors %u/%u, flag0 %#x, %u bytes, at gain %u%%\n", device,
               motors[DUALSENSE_USB_COMMON + DUALSENSE_COMMON_MOTOR_RIGHT],
               motors[DUALSENSE_USB_COMMON + DUALSENSE_COMMON_MOTOR_LEFT],
@@ -2064,6 +2097,24 @@
      * IOCTL still takes the device lock, the event still round-trips wineserver,
      * this thread still wakes, still coalesces, still builds the packet. Only
      * the last step does not happen. */
+    /* MGVF mgvf-0018: the smallest change worth a packet.
+     *
+     * Measured on a Bluetooth session of an Unreal title, 1951 motor packets:
+     * 49% of them moved a motor by ONE step of 255, and 76% by four or fewer.
+     * A step is 0.4% of the motor's range. Nobody feels one; every one of them
+     * costs a slot on a link that carries about 65 reports a second.
+     *
+     * This is not the interval by another name, and the difference is the whole
+     * point. The interval drops changes by TIME and cannot tell a hammer blow
+     * from a tremble, which is why raising it makes rumble feel dead. This drops
+     * changes by SIZE: every transition a hand can notice goes out at once, and
+     * what is held back is dithering. */
+    ext->haptics_deadband = get_device_option(ext->desc.vid, ext->desc.pid, L"XInputRumbleDeadband", 0);
+    if (ext->haptics_deadband > 255) ext->haptics_deadband = 255;
+    if (ext->haptics_deadband)
+        TRACE("device %p: a motor must move %u of 255 before the link is used\n",
+              device, ext->haptics_deadband);
+
     ext->haptics_dry_run = get_device_option(ext->desc.vid, ext->desc.pid, L"XInputRumbleDryRun", 0);
     if (ext->haptics_dry_run)
         ERR("device %p: XInputRumbleDryRun is set -- the motors will be built and NOT written. "
